## 12. IMAGE SEO: FILENAMES, ALT TEXT, AND COMPRESSION

Optimize every meaningful article image.

### Image filenames

Use descriptive, lowercase filenames separated by hyphens.

Good example:
monthly-budget-planning-example.webp

Avoid:
IMG_2031.jpg

When renaming an existing image, update every reference to that image so nothing breaks.

### Alt text

Every meaningful image must have descriptive alt text that accurately explains its content or purpose.

Example:

<img src="/images/monthly-budget-planning-example.webp" alt="Example of a monthly budget showing income, expenses, and savings">

Requirements:

- Write concise, natural descriptions.
- Include a keyword only when it accurately describes the image.
- Do not stuff keywords into alt text.
- Do not use the same generic alt text for every image.
- Use an empty alt attribute for purely decorative images.
- Do not invent details that cannot be seen in the image.
- Preserve appropriate image dimensions and responsive behavior.

### Image compression

- Prefer optimized WebP or AVIF when supported.
- Compress unnecessarily large image files.
- Use responsive images and appropriate image dimensions.
- Include width and height attributes or equivalent aspect-ratio handling to reduce layout shifts.
- Lazy-load below-the-fold images.
- Do not lazy-load the primary above-the-fold or likely Largest Contentful Paint image unnecessarily.
- Use suitable fallbacks for unsupported image formats.

Do not remove images or degrade their quality unnecessarily.

## 13. INTERNAL LINKS AND DESCRIPTIVE ANCHOR TEXT

Every article must contain approximately two or three relevant internal links when suitable destination pages exist.

Internal links must point to other useful pages on the same website, such as related articles, relevant category pages, or useful guides.

### Anchor text requirements

Anchor text is the clickable text readers see.

Use descriptive anchor text that tells readers what the destination page covers.

Weak example:
"For more information, click here."

Strong example:
"Learn how to create a monthly budget."

Another strong example:
"Understand the difference between saving and investing."

Requirements:

- Use descriptive, relevant anchor text for all meaningful internal links.
- Avoid vague phrases such as "click here," "read more," and "this page" when a more descriptive phrase is possible.
- Avoid using the exact same keyword-rich anchor for every link to a destination.
- Link naturally within relevant paragraphs.
- Link only to pages that exist.
- Use valid, crawlable HTML anchor elements.
- Avoid unnecessary JavaScript-only navigation.
- Check all internal links for broken routes.
- Ensure internal links point to the correct canonical URLs.

If no suitable destination exists, do not invent a URL. Flag the missing destination or create a genuinely useful page when it fits the website's content plan.

